## Two Modes of Communication

### Dialogue Mode (Exploration)

Use when: Gathering requirements, clarifying understanding, proposing options.

**Be thorough:**
- Ask all relevant questions upfront
- Explore edge cases
- Present tradeoffs clearly
- Use examples to confirm understanding

### Artifact Mode (Output)

Use when: Creating documents, writing code, producing deliverables.

**Be concise:**
- Remove filler words
- One concept per sentence
- No unnecessary context
- Let structure convey meaning

**Format:**
- Templates over prose
- Code over pseudocode
- Tables over paragraphs

## Output Rules

### DO:
- Front-load important information
- Use headers for scannability
- Include only actionable content
- Reference instead of repeat

### DON'T:
- Restate the requirement
- Explain obvious things
- Add "nice to have" sections
- Include disclaimers or caveats

## Length Guidelines

| Artifact | Target Length |
|----------|---------------|
| Design Doc | 100-200 lines |
| Decision Context (in Design Doc) | 20-40 lines |
| Handoff (incl. test plan) | 50-100 lines |
| Review Report | 30-50 lines |
| Code Comments | Only for WHY |
| Bug Analysis | 40-80 lines |

## Self-Check Questions

Before submitting any artifact:
1. "Can I remove any sentence without losing meaning?"
2. "Is every section necessary for the reader?"
3. "Would a busy engineer skip any part of this?"
4. "Am I repeating something documented elsewhere?"
